==========================================================================================================
LINUX /etc/ DIRECTORY CONFIGURATION FILES REFERENCE GUIDE
==========================================================================================================

This document provides a concise reference guide to the core configuration, 
system, and initialization files typically found inside the Linux /etc/ directory.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. TIME & SCHEDULING
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- adjtime: Stores calibration data and adjustments made to correct system clock drift.

- anacrontab: Configures anacron for running periodic maintenance jobs even if the system was powered off.

- chrony.conf: Main configuration file for the chronyd NTP daemon, defining time sources and sync rules.

- chrony.keys: Contains authentication keys used by Chrony for secure command-line or network communication.

- cron.deny: Specifies users forbidden from creating or using automated cron jobs.

- crontab: System-wide configuration file defining scheduled cron jobs.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. USER MANAGEMENT & SECURITY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- group / group-: Defines system groups (- acts as a backup file during updates).

- gshadow / gshadow-: Manages secure group passwords and encrypted admin group settings.

- passwd / passwd-: Stores core user account information like usernames, UIDs, and shell paths.

- shadow / shadow-: Holds encrypted user account passwords and expiration policies.

- subgid / subgid-: Defines subordinate group ID ranges used for rootless containerization (e.g., Docker, Podman).

- subuid / subuid-: Defines subordinate user ID ranges for rootless containers and namespaces.

- sudoers / sudo.conf: Manages administrative sudo privileges and access security plugins.

- crypttab: Lists encrypted file systems, block devices, or partitions (like LUKS volumes) to unlock at boot.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. NETWORK & DNS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- ethertypes: Maps Ethernet protocol names to their numeric packet types.

- exports: Configures directories shared over the network via NFS (Network File System).

- host.conf: Determines how the operating system resolves hostnames.

- hostname: Contains the single text string defining the system's network hostname.

- hosts: A static mapping of hostnames to IP addresses for local name resolution.

- networks: Maps network names to network IP addresses.

- nsswitch.conf / nsswitch.conf.bak: Configures Name Service Switch lookup order for users, groups, and hosts.

- protocols: Lists standard network protocols (TCP, UDP, ICMP) and their numbers.

- resolv.conf: Sets IP addresses of DNS servers used for domain name resolution.

- rpc: Maps Remote Procedure Call (RPC) program numbers to names.

- services: Maps network service names to assigned port numbers and protocols.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. SYSTEM INITIALIZATION & BOOT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- dracut.conf: Configuration file for Dracut, used to generate initial RAM disk (initramfs) images.

- fstab: File Systems Table listing disk partitions and mount points for automated mounting at boot.

- grub2.cfg: Main bootloader configuration file generated by GRUB2, controlling boot options.

- inittab: Legacy configuration file for SysVinit, determining system default runlevel or target.

- machine-id: Unique machine identifier generated for every individual Linux installation.

- os-release / almalinux-release / redhat-release / system-release: Text files with OS version and ID metadata.

- rc.local: Legacy startup script executed at the end of the traditional boot sequence.

- sysctl.conf: Defines kernel parameters to tweak performance, memory, networking, and security.

- vconsole.conf: Configures virtual console settings (keyboard layouts and console fonts).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. ENVIRONMENT & SHELL CONFIGURATION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- bashrc: Shell configuration script loaded for interactive Bash shell sessions.

- csh.cshrc / csh.login: System-wide configuration scripts for the C shell (csh).

- environment: Sets system-wide environment variables available to all processes.

- inputrc: Configures readline library behavior (keyboard shortcuts and editing modes).

- profile: Sets default shell variables and startup environment settings for users.

- virc: Default configuration settings for the vi text editor.

- shells: Lists valid login shells allowed on the system.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. APPEARANCE, STYLING & LOCALIZATION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- DIR_COLORS / DIR_COLORS.lightbgcolor: Defines color themes for directory listings in ls.

- GREP_COLORS: Defines default highlighting colors used by the grep search utility.

- locale.conf: Sets system-wide language, country, and character encoding settings.

- localtime: Symlink pointing to the system's active timezone file in /usr/share/zoneinfo/.

- issue / issue.net: Pre-login greeting messages displayed on local terminals or SSH connections.

- motd: Message of the Day text displayed to users immediately after successful login.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. LOGGING, UTILITIES & OTHER CONFIGURATIONS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- kdump.conf: Configures kernel crash dump behavior for capturing debugging data.

- krb5.conf: Configuration settings for Kerberos 5 authentication and domain realms.

- ld.so.cache / ld.so.conf: Manages shared library paths loaded dynamically by applications.

- libaudit.conf: Configuration for the Linux audit subsystem tracking security events.

- libuser.conf: Default configurations for user and group management libraries.

- login.defs: Sets shadow password suite parameters (expiration limits, default UID ranges).

- logrotate.conf: Manages rotation, compression, and archival of system log files.

- magic: Defines file magic numbers used by the file command to identify file types.

- man_db.conf: Configures search paths and behavior for manual (man) pages.

- mke2fs.conf: Configuration parameters for creating ext2, ext3, and ext4 filesystems.

- mtab: Dynamic file tracking currently mounted filesystems.

- printcap: Legacy printer capability database.

- rsyslog.conf: Main configuration file for the rsyslog system logging daemon.

- sestatus.conf: Configuration and tracking settings for SELinux.

- yum.conf: Main configuration file for legacy YUM package manager.
